WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.800
Welcome back to our deep dive.

00:00:01.800 --> 00:00:05.680
So today we're digging into a pretty massive paradox

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:06.880
in the US health care system.

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:08.020
Yeah, it's a huge one.

00:00:08.020 --> 00:00:09.680
Right, because our mission today

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:13.040
is to figure out how Puerto Rico built this

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:15.360
like high performance pharmacy engine

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:18.240
that completely beats the national average

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:20.520
and you know why the federal government

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:23.400
is basically forcing them to drive it on an empty tank.

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:25.360
Well, to really understand that paradox

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:27.760
we have to look at how Medicare Advantage plan

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:31.000
funding actually works, you know, under the hood.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:32.560
Which comes down to the star ratings, right?

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:33.800
Exactly.

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:38.320
Those stars dictate how much federal funding a plan gets

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:39.880
and they are heavily, heavily based

00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:41.400
on patient medication adherence.

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:43.640
Meaning are people actually taking their meds?

00:00:43.640 --> 00:00:44.240
Right.

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:45.640
If patients aren't consistently

00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:48.640
taking their prescriptions for chronic issues,

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:51.560
things like hypertension, diabetes, or high cholesterol,

00:00:51.560 --> 00:00:54.000
well, the plan on a star rating drops

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:55.320
and then the funding just dries up.

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:58.120
Wow, OK, so jump back to 2013.

00:00:58.120 --> 00:01:00.280
The community pharmacies in Puerto Rico,

00:01:00.280 --> 00:01:02.200
they actually saw this tension early on.

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:03.680
They did, it was really visionary.

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:06.080
Yeah, specifically the asociación de farmacías

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:09.200
de comunidad de Puerto Rico, or AFCPR.

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:10.800
Under the presidency of Elliot Pacheco,

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:12.320
their board of directors realized

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:14.760
they needed a real systemic fix.

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:16.800
And that's where the adherence program was born.

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:19.080
I mean, the clinical protocol itself

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:22.280
was elaborated by doctors of pharmacy Nuri Echeveria

00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:25.960
and Vixima Rodriguez, along with Elto Ricardo-Cintron.

00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:27.800
Right, and the crazy part is they didn't just keep it

00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:28.640
to themselves.

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:31.000
No, they took this unified model

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:34.240
straight to the Medicare Advantage plans to implement,

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:35.960
which is a massive achievement.

00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:39.560
They identified the gap and completely shifted the focus

00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:42.040
from just handing over pills

00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:44.640
to actively managing the patient's care.

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:47.240
I actually like to think of it like a racing pit crew.

00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:48.400
Oh, that's a good way to put it.

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:49.720
Right, like instead of a patient

00:01:49.720 --> 00:01:51.240
making five different trips a month

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:53.240
and inevitably forgetting a pill,

00:01:53.240 --> 00:01:55.360
these pharmacists act like the pit crew.

00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:57.440
They do medication synchronization.

00:01:57.440 --> 00:01:58.880
They do reconciliation.

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:00.600
Making sure there are no bad drug interactions.

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:02.360
Exactly, they get recognized

00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:04.920
and paid for clinically tuning the engine,

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:07.520
not just handing the driver a box of spare parts.

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:11.080
And the federal health data from 2020 to 2023

00:02:11.080 --> 00:02:12.800
shows us exactly how well

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:16.360
that pit crew approach actually works in practice.

00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:17.760
The numbers are pretty wild.

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:18.720
They really are.

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:23.200
Island residents fill about 19% more prescriptions annually

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:24.880
than the national benchmark.

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:26.400
Which, you know, you'd assume

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:27.720
that kind of intensive care

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:29.600
would drive costs through the roof.

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:30.760
But it doesn't.

00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:34.640
They're paying roughly 30% less per fill

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:35.880
than the national average.

00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:38.760
We are talking about $63 per prescription

00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:40.960
versus $89 nationally.

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:43.720
Wait, so they are ranked first among all 56

00:02:43.720 --> 00:02:46.560
U.S. jurisdictions for highest utilization

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:48.520
and lowest gross drug costs.

00:02:48.520 --> 00:02:49.840
Yes, exactly.

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:51.680
Number one out of all 56.

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:53.680
It's a staggering dual achievement.

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:55.840
And, you know, we have to be really precise here.

00:02:55.840 --> 00:02:57.880
This isn't just patients buying meds

00:02:57.880 --> 00:03:00.280
because they have a lower price tag, not at all.

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:01.440
Right, it's highly deliberate.

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:02.800
Yeah, the pharmacy profile there

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:04.960
focuses heavily on generic treatments

00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:07.760
for chronic conditions like thyroid and heart health.

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:10.480
It is fundamentally a culture of high medical adherence.

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:12.200
Which brings us right back to the paradox

00:03:12.200 --> 00:03:13.440
you mentioned at the start.

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:14.480
The empty tank.

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:15.320
Exactly.

00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:18.160
You had the most efficient, highly adherent jurisdiction

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:22.080
in the country hitting these five-star performance levels.

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:24.480
But because of statutory limitations,

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:27.800
Puerto Rico is legally boxed out of the low-income subsidies

00:03:27.800 --> 00:03:29.760
that residents in the other 50 states

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:31.080
just get automatically.

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:32.000
Hold on.

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:34.280
I mean, if they are ranked number one

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:37.000
for keeping costs low and adherence high,

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:38.720
shouldn't they be drowning in federal support?

00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:39.880
You would think so.

00:03:39.880 --> 00:03:42.000
But it's a statutory blind spot.

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:43.960
The irony is sharp, honestly.

00:03:43.960 --> 00:03:46.000
The actual foregone assistance,

00:03:46.040 --> 00:03:49.560
meaning the money denied to these low-income patients,

00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:52.440
is estimated at $250 million annually.

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:54.000
$250 million, wow.

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:57.120
Yeah, and if you look at potential parity funding,

00:03:57.120 --> 00:03:58.600
treating them exactly like a state

00:03:58.600 --> 00:04:00.880
that a figure could reach up to $2 billion.

00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:02.960
So what does this mean for you listening, right,

00:04:02.960 --> 00:04:04.920
as someone navigating the health care system?

00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:08.760
It proves that this proactive community-level care works.

00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:10.480
It gives us a clear blueprint.

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:11.440
It does.

00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:14.480
And honestly, it serves as a firm recommendation

00:04:14.480 --> 00:04:15.720
to the federal government.

00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:18.080
They need to acknowledge how local providers

00:04:18.080 --> 00:04:20.280
in Puerto Rico successfully work together

00:04:20.280 --> 00:04:23.160
for the benefit of the patients to reach those five stars.

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:24.000
Absolutely.

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:26.120
It shouldn't be some underfunded anomaly.

00:04:26.120 --> 00:04:28.320
It needs to be used as a national model.

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:31.160
And it's a strong recommendation to health plans,

00:04:31.160 --> 00:04:32.440
to other health care providers,

00:04:32.440 --> 00:04:35.480
and to patients to continue this proactive collaboration.

00:04:35.480 --> 00:04:38.520
Yeah, you have to keep the pit crew talking to the driver.

00:04:38.520 --> 00:04:41.160
So I will leave you with this final thought to mellow over.

00:04:41.160 --> 00:04:44.160
Consider what staggering heights of health care efficiency

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:46.000
could be reached nationwide

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:48.200
if local on-the-ground innovation

00:04:48.200 --> 00:04:51.280
was actually matched by equitable federal funding.

00:04:51.280 --> 00:04:53.080
If it runs this well on empty,

00:04:53.080 --> 00:04:55.280
imagine what it could do with a full tank.

